    {
      "id": "src_4e833c347043",
      "title": "France's Solar Car Park Law: A Model for UK Businesses - LinkedIn",
      "url": "https://www.linkedin.com/posts/davidjswift_trump-solarenergy-renewables-activity-7446455340390510592-pdsN#",
      "snippet": "In 2023 France passed a landmark law requiring all large outdoor car parks (80+ spaces) to install solar canopies covering at least 50% of",
      "content": "Don’t cover our fields. Cover our car parks. France proved it can be done and done well. In 2023 France passed a landmark law requiring all large outdoor car parks (80+ spaces) to install solar canopies covering at least 50% of the surface. Larger sites (>10,000 m²) have until July 2026; smaller ones until 2028. The projected outcome? Up to 11 GW of new solar capacity without touching a single hectare of productive farmland. Solar is now the cheapest form of new electricity globally. Yet with energy prices climbing again, driven in part by geopolitical shocks, including disruptions from the recent conflict in the Middle East tied to #Trump administration actions the vulnerability of our fossil-heavy grids is painfully clear. Why expose businesses and households to volatile oil and gas prices when we have millions of square metres of sun-drenched, already-developed parking sitting idle? The triple win of solar carports: ✅ Clean, stable power generated right where it’s needed (shopping centres, offices, hospitals, logistics parks) shielding against sudden spikes in wholesale energy costs ✅ Natural shade for vehicles cooler cars in summer, less air-con, lower charging costs for EVs, and protection from rain, hail and snow ✅ Zero new land take preserving fields for food, nature, and future generations Here in the UK we’re moving in the right direction with new Permitted Development Rights for solar canopies and live consultations on mandating them on new car parks. But in a world of rising energy bills and supply uncertainty, we need to accelerate. Every new warehouse, retail park, or office development should come with solar overhead as standard. Businesses that act now will lock in cheaper, more predictable electricity, boost their ESG credentials, and future-proof against the next price shock. The question is simple: Why sacrifice fertile soil - or accept volatile energy costs - when we can turn dead asphalt into reliable power stations? Let’s be like France. Let’s put solar where it actually belongs. What’s your take? Have you seen (or installed) solar canopies on a car park near you? Or are you involved in a project making this happen? How are rising energy costs affecting your operations? Drop your thoughts or examples below. #SolarEnergy #Renewables #NetZero #EnergyCosts #SustainableInfrastructure #EVCharging #EnergySecurity France's Solar Car Park Law: A Model for UK Businesses",
      "source_type": "web",
      "provider": "tavily",
      "providers": [
        "tavily"
      ],
